{"name":"Yippie Yellow","hex":"#ffff84","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"yippie","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of the Youth International Party, a group of politically active hippies."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Alternative form of yippee."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yippie"},{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Youth International Party","blocks":[{"text":"The Youth International Party (YIP), commonly called the Yippies, was an American youth-oriented radical and countercultural revolutionary offshoot of the free speech and anti-war movements of the late 1960s. It was founded on December 31, 1967. They employed theatrical gestures to mock the social status quo, such as advancing a pig called \"Pigasus the Immortal\" as a candidate for president of the United States in 1968. They have been described as a highly theatrical, anti-authoritarian, and anarchist youth movement of \"symbolic politics\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Flag_of_Yippies.svg/330px-Flag_of_Yippies.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Youth_International_Party"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c9/Yippie_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c9/Yippie_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"YIP poster advertising the 1968 Festival of Life.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYippie_poster.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Yippie%21_Button_at_the_Chicago_History_Museum.jpg/500px-Yippie%21_Button_at_the_Chicago_History_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Yippie%21_Button_at_the_Chicago_History_Museum.jpg/1280px-Yippie%21_Button_at_the_Chicago_History_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Yippie%21_Button_at_the_Chicago_History_Museum.jpg/500px-Yippie%21_Button_at_the_Chicago_History_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_c